\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The finite surface character formula}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the [surface data and coefficients](fdip-006V),
##{Z_\Sigma(f)=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}
 d_\rho^{\,2-2g}\left(\frac{c_\rho}{d_\rho}\right)^P.}
The formula uses normalized counting on every edge. In particular,
#{f\equiv1} gives #{Z_\Sigma(1)=1}; the unnormalized sum is
#{|G|^{|E|}}.}

\proof{
\p{First prove the required matrix orthogonality. For irreducible
unitary representations #{\rho,\tau} and a linear map
#{A:V_\tau\to V_\rho}, let
##{\mathcal A(A)=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{h\in G}
 \rho(h)A\tau(h)^{-1}.}
Changing the summation variable shows this is an intertwiner. Schur's
lemma makes it zero when the representatives are inequivalent. For
#{\rho=\tau}, it is scalar, and preservation of trace gives
#{\mathcal A(A)=(\operatorname{tr}A/d_\rho)I}. Apply this to matrix
units to obtain
##{\frac1{|G|}\sum_{h\in G}
 \rho(h)_{ij}\tau(h^{-1})_{kl}
 =\begin{cases}
 d_\rho^{-1}\delta_{il}\delta_{jk},&\rho=\tau,\\
 0,&\rho\ne\tau.
 \end{cases}}
This is a finite averaging argument; all sums below are finite.}

\p{Expand #{f} on every face and each character as a matrix trace
along the ordered boundary word. Label every face by an irreducible
representation and every corner by the corresponding matrix index.
An edge occurs in two face traces, once as #{\rho(U_e)} and once
as #{\tau(U_e^{-1})}. Averaging that edge gives the displayed
orthogonality identity. If the adjacent faces differ, their labels
must coincide for a nonzero contribution. The dual adjacency graph
of a connected closed surface is connected, so one common label
#{\rho} remains on every face. A self-adjacent edge simply contracts
two occurrences of this same label. Each edge contributes
#{d_\rho^{-1}}.}

\p{The two Kronecker deltas identify corner indices at the same
endpoint of that edge. Around a vertex, the successive sectors form
its circular link. Consequently all corners at that vertex have one
common index, while no contraction equates indices at different
vertices. Exactly one free index remains per vertex, contributing
#{d_\rho^{|V|}}. This also covers loops and repeated face edges:
the circular link, rather than distinct face or edge names, determines
the index cycle. The contribution of the common label is therefore
##{c_\rho^P d_\rho^{|V|-|E|}
 =d_\rho^{2-2g}\left(\frac{c_\rho}{d_\rho}\right)^P,}
using #{|V|-|E|+P=2-2g}. Sum over #{\rho}. For #{f=1}, character
orthogonality makes the trivial coefficient one and all others zero.}
}

\p{The exceptional CW description of a sphere with one vertex, no
edges and one face attached by a constant map lies outside the
nonempty polygon convention. Its partition sum is directly #{f(1)}.
The same expression still holds, since #{g=0}, #{P=1} and
#{f(1)=\sum_\rho d_\rho c_\rho}.}

\p{For #{G=\{1,-1\}} and #{f(h)=e^{-\beta(1-h)}}, the two
coefficients are #{c_+=e^{-\beta}\cosh\beta} and
#{c_-=e^{-\beta}\sinh\beta}. On the torus the normalized sum is
#{c_+^P+c_-^P}. Multiplication by #{2^{|E|}} recovers the
[exact square-torus partition function](fdip-006R), since
#{|E|=2P}. For a disk of #{A} faces, insertion of its boundary
Wilson sign multiplies the face weight by the sign on each interior
face. This swaps #{c_+} and #{c_-} there. The same edge contraction
then gives numerator
##{c_-^A c_+^{P-A}+c_+^A c_-^{P-A}.}
Division by the partition sum recovers
#{(t^A+t^{P-A})/(1+t^P)}, with #{t=\tanh\beta}, under either
counting convention.}

\p{For character convolution with normalized group integration, see
equation (9) in \citet{p. 414}{migdal1975recursion}.
Witten's Section 2.3 uses normalized Haar measure and character
contractions in equations (2.44), (2.48), (2.50)–(2.51)
\citet{pp. 163–166}{witten1991quantum}. His continuum construction uses
heat-kernel face weights with area-dependent Casimir factors.
The proof here is for a finite group and an arbitrary fixed central
face weight. It does not assert subdivision invariance for the Wilson
weight or transfer continuum conclusions to these finite models.}
